CAT = "rsracCtlService"; SEG = "CTS"
Q = [
("A thermostat calls for cooling but the contactor never pulls in. What is measured first at the contactor coil?",
 "24 V across the coil", ["Line voltage at the compressor", "Refrigerant pressure", "Resistance of the run winding"],
 "If 24 V reaches the coil and it does not pull in, the coil is faulty. If no voltage arrives, the fault is upstream in the control circuit: transformer, fuse, safeties or wiring."),
("A control transformer's secondary reads 0 V while its primary has 120 V. What is likely?",
 "Open transformer or blown secondary fuse", ["Shorted thermostat battery", "Low refrigerant charge", "Stuck reversing valve"],
 "With supply on the primary and nothing on the secondary, the transformer winding or its secondary fuse has opened, often from a short in the control wiring."),
("How can a temperature sensor's accuracy be checked in the field?",
 "Ice bath at 0 C", ["Read it with the power off", "Heat it with a torch", "Measure its supply amperage"],
 "A well-stirred ice and water mix sits at 0 C. Comparing the sensor reading with it, or with a calibrated reference thermometer, shows its offset."),
("A thermistor reads 10 kilohms at 25 C, and its resistance falls as it warms. What type is it?",
 "Negative temperature coefficient", ["Positive temperature coefficient", "Thermocouple junction", "Resistance temperature detector"],
 "An NTC thermistor's resistance drops as temperature rises. Makers publish a resistance-temperature table, so the reading at a known temperature checks the sensor."),
("A replacement sensor has the same connector but a different resistance curve. What happens if it is installed?",
 "Wrong temperature readings", ["It works the same way", "The fuse blows at once", "It reads correctly after an hour"],
 "The controller converts resistance using the original sensor's curve. A different curve makes every reading wrong, so a sensor is replaced with the maker's matching part."),
("How is a high-pressure safety cut-out tested on an operating system?",
 "Raise head pressure to trip it", ["Jumper it and run the unit", "Unplug the condenser fan for a day", "Read its setting from the box only"],
 "The safety is proven by slowly raising head pressure, for example by limiting condenser airflow under supervision, and confirming it opens at its setting. Jumpering a safety defeats it."),
("On a pressure control, what does the differential setting determine?",
 "Cut-in to cut-out gap", ["Maximum system pressure", "Compressor speed", "Refrigerant type"],
 "The differential is how far pressure must change between the switch opening and closing. Too small a differential short cycles the compressor."),
("A defrost board never starts a defrost and the outdoor coil stays iced. What is tested on the board's inputs?",
 "Coil sensor", ["Indoor fan speed", "Thermostat Y2 wire", "Liquid line pressure"],
 "Demand defrost boards start a cycle when the coil sensor shows frosting conditions. An open or misplaced sensor keeps the board from ever calling for defrost."),
("On a ladder wiring diagram, how are safety switches usually drawn in a compressor control circuit?",
 "In series with the contactor coil", ["In parallel with the coil", "On the line-voltage side only", "Across the transformer primary"],
 "Safeties are wired in series so any one opening breaks the circuit to the contactor coil and stops the compressor."),
("A relay coil rated for 24 V reads zero ohms. What does that indicate?",
 "Shorted coil", ["Normal coil", "Open coil", "Coil needing higher voltage"],
 "A good coil has a measurable resistance. Zero ohms means a short that would overload the transformer; an open coil reads infinite."),
("A building automation system shows a sensor at -40 C on a summer day. What is the likely fault?",
 "Open sensor circuit", ["Very cold supply air", "Normal reading", "Overcharged chiller"],
 "Many controllers show an extreme value at the end of their range when the sensor wire is open or the sensor has failed. The wiring and sensor are checked."),
("Why is a firmware update for a variable frequency drive done to the maker's instructions?",
 "A failed update can disable the drive", ["Updates change the motor size", "It resets the refrigerant charge", "Firmware sets the line voltage"],
 "Interrupting an update or loading the wrong file can leave a drive inoperable. Parameters are backed up first and the maker's procedure is followed."),
("During control maintenance, why are the setpoints in a controller compared with the commissioning record?",
 "To find drifted changes", ["To change the refrigerant", "To measure airflow", "To test the overloads"],
 "Setpoints changed by occupants or after power faults can waste energy or cause comfort complaints. Comparing with the record shows what has changed."),
("An electronic expansion valve stays shut even though the controller calls for it to open. What is checked first?",
 "Coil and drive signal", ["Condenser fan blade pitch", "Crankcase heater", "Thermostat location"],
 "An EEV is driven by a stepper motor coil from the controller. A loose connector, damaged coil or missing drive signal leaves it closed."),
("A low-pressure switch keeps tripping on a cold-room unit during light load. What adjustment may be needed?",
 "Compare cut-out to design", ["Bypass it until summer", "Remove the switch", "Raise the head pressure setting only"],
 "The switch's cut-out may be set too high for the box temperature. It is compared with the refrigerant's saturation pressure at the coil's lowest design temperature before any change."),
("Why is a calibrated reference used when checking a humidity sensor?",
 "They drift with age and contamination", ["Humidity sensors cannot fail", "It sets the dew point", "It powers the sensor"],
 "Relative humidity sensors drift with age and contamination. A calibrated reference shows the offset so it can be corrected or the sensor replaced."),
]
